Tracked by #67299, implemented in #68129.

## Background and Motivation

WebAuthn has [signal methods](https://www.w3.org/TR/webauthn-3/#sctn-signal-methods) that let a site tell the browser's passkey provider what it currently knows about a user. Without them, a passkey deleted on the server keeps being offered at sign-in forever, and a changed username stays stale next to the passkey.

Two of the three signals need the same data, and an app can't easily assemble it. The user handle isn't the user ID, it's the UTF-8 bytes of the ID base64url encoded through the internal `BufferSource`, and the RP ID may be overridden via `IdentityPasskeyOptions.ServerDomain`. Get either wrong and the browser silently matches nothing, so the app sees success while the passkey stays visible.

The third signal, `signalUnknownCredential`, is out of scope here. It needs a different payload and a way to detect an unknown credential during sign-in, tracked in #68128.

## Proposed API

```diff
 namespace Microsoft.AspNetCore.Identity;

 public interface IPasskeyHandler<TUser> where TUser : class
 {
+    bool SupportsSignalOptions => false;
+
+    Task<PasskeySignalOptionsResult> MakeSignalOptionsAsync(TUser user, PasskeyUserEntity userEntity, HttpContext httpContext)
+        => throw new NotSupportedException($"'{GetType()}' does not support generating passkey signal options.");
 }

 public sealed class PasskeyHandler<TUser> : IPasskeyHandler<TUser> where TUser : class
 {
+    public bool SupportsSignalOptions { get; }
+    public Task<PasskeySignalOptionsResult> MakeSignalOptionsAsync(TUser user, PasskeyUserEntity userEntity, HttpContext httpContext);
 }

+public sealed class PasskeySignalOptionsResult
+{
+    public required string SignalOptionsJson { get; init; }
+}

 public class SignInManager<TUser> where TUser : class
 {
+    public virtual bool SupportsPasskeySignalOptions { get; }
+    public virtual Task<string> MakePasskeySignalOptionsAsync(TUser user, PasskeyUserEntity userEntity);
 }
```
The work goes on `IPasskeyHandler<TUser>` because that's where RP ID resolution already lives for creation and assertion, so a custom handler signals the same RP ID it issued. `SignInManager` keeps a thin wrapper, matching `MakePasskeyCreationOptionsAsync` and `MakePasskeyRequestOptionsAsync`, since apps hold a `SignInManager` and not a handler.

## Usage Examples

Server side, in the Blazor Web App template's manage passkeys page:

```csharp
if (SignInManager.SupportsPasskeySignalOptions)
{
    signalOptionsJson = await SignInManager.MakePasskeySignalOptionsAsync(user, new()
    {
        Id = userId,
        Name = userName,
        DisplayName = userName,
    });
}
```

Client side, the one payload feeds both calls:

```js
const { rpId, userId, allAcceptedCredentialIds, name, displayName } = JSON.parse(signalOptionsJson);
await PublicKeyCredential.signalAllAcceptedCredentials?.({ rpId, userId, allAcceptedCredentialIds });
await PublicKeyCredential.signalCurrentUserDetails?.({ rpId, userId, name, displayName });
```

## Alternative Designs

**A separate `IPasskeySignalOptionsHandler<TUser>` interface** instead of a default interface method plus `SupportsSignalOptions`. Support would then be a type check rather than a flag the handler reports about itself, which matches `UserManager.SupportsUserPasskey` (`Store is IUserPasskeyStore<TUser>`) and can't be got wrong. The cost is a second interface for one method. I went with the default method for that reason, but the other shape is fine by me if the review prefers it.

**Two ready-made payloads instead of one superset.** `signalAllAcceptedCredentials` and `signalCurrentUserDetails` overlap on `rpId` and `userId`, so one object covers both and the JS destructures it. Returning two objects would be harder to misuse but duplicates fields and doubles the call sites, and both signals should fire together anyway.

**Naming with `signalUnknownCredential` in mind.** That one takes `{ rpId, credentialId }`, has no user, and is called before sign-in, so it can't share this payload whatever I call it. I'd give it its own method and keep `MakeSignalOptionsAsync` for the two authenticated signals.


## Risks

`IPasskeyHandler<TUser>` shipped in .NET 10, so the new member is a default interface method to avoid breaking existing implementations. Those handlers inherit the throwing default, which is why `SupportsSignalOptions` exists: callers check it first, and the built-in `PasskeyHandler` returns `true`.

A handler that implements the method but forgets to override `SupportsSignalOptions` gets a silent no-op. The separate-interface alternative above removes that.

Returning a JSON string rather than a typed object matches the existing passkey methods, but it means the shape is documented rather than enforced.

